    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The first member of the family whose digits are all different is \\( N = 11 \\).\n\nIf the first number of the family is \\( N_1 = 1111, \\) then the other numbers of the same sequence (\\( N_2, N_3, \\ldots \\)) will be related to the common factor, therefore, will start with \\( N' = 1111 \\), which is not a palindrome number.\nIf the first number of the family is bigger than 1111, then \\( N = 11^{25} \\), otherwise \\( N' = 11 \\cdot 11^{24} \\), but because \\( 11^{25} = 11111 \\ldots (26 \\text{ ones}) \\), which is not a palindrome number, the largest number of the family the same sequence will be \\( N_2 = N' \\), therefore \\( N = 1 \\times 11^{199} = 11^{199} \\).\nTherefore, the answer is \\( 11^{199} \\).",
